#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1020.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176766} :activity/id #uuid "78ca9e7b-ee37-50b5-a0a2-23ce70dad177" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050502 :topic/id #uuid "4e4e9d7a-bee2-5790-9304-d063d47d2ed1" :topic/math-academy-id 3640} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176766 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177010 :tutorial/content "The **joint cumulative distribution function** (or joint CDF) of two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is defined as\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X \\leq x,Y \\leq y).\n$$\n\nThe joint CDF gives the probability that the pair $(X,Y)$ will lie inside the infinite rectangular region $\\Delta$ that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axes.\n\n![](images/ee/ee82c6b810f02f8b8380bd97c94cbe491095d17dbadc685ffdca1a54cf9828b8.png)\n\nThe joint CDF satisfies the following properties:\n\n- $0\\leq F(x,y)\\leq 1$\n\n- The **marginal CDF** of $X$ is given by $F_X(x)=F(x,\\infty).$\n\n- The **marginal CDF** of $Y$ is given by $F_Y(y)=F(\\infty,y).$\n\n- $F(\\infty, \\infty)=1$\n\n- $F(-\\infty ,y)=F(x,-\\infty)=0$\n\n- If $X$ and $Y$ are independent random variables, then \n$F(x,y)=F_X(x)\\cdot F_Y(y).$\n\nIf $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables with joint probability density function $f(x, y)$, we have the following relationship between the joint PDF and joint CDF:\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\int^y_{-\\infty}\\int^x_{-\\infty} f(u,v)\\; \\textrm{d}u\\textrm{d}v.\n$$\n\nConsequently, if $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables that are nonzero only over the rectangle $[a, b] \\times [c, d]$, then the joint CDF is \n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\int^d_{c}\\int^b_{a} f(u,v)\\; \\textrm{d}u\\textrm{d}v.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "891e5619-fe8a-5703-8683-51845abfa85f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5541 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "b7b060dd-8145-5ed5-a8c2-a8eb95b625a3" :step/math-academy-id 34404 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176771}} {:db/id #edb/ref 1759